/* ===========================================================================
   tap_qsp_model.dot
   Toxic alcohol poisoning — methanol and ethylene glycol — mechanistic QSP map
   독성 알코올 중독 (메탄올 · 에틸렌글리콜) 기계론적 QSP 지도

   ORGANISING THESIS (see README.md):

     The parent alcohol is not the poison.  Methanol and ethylene glycol are,
     to a first approximation, ethanol-like sedatives with an unusually large
     osmotic footprint and no organ toxicity of their own.  ALL of the injury
     lives in an acid metabolite that the patient's own liver manufactures:

         methanol         --ADH-->  formaldehyde --ALDH-->  FORMATE
         ethylene glycol  --ADH-->  glycolaldehyde -->  GLYCOLATE --> OXALATE

     Therefore the disease is not a concentration.  It is an INTEGRAL of a
     FLUX, and the flux is a single Michaelis-Menten expression in which every
     therapy ever used owns exactly one term:

                            Vmax * P
       d(ACID)/dt  =  ------------------------------      <-- FORMATION
                       Km*(1 + F/Ki + E/Ke) + P               fomepizole (F)
                                                              ethanol (E) own
                                                              the DENOMINATOR

                      - CLr * f_ion(pH_urine) * ACID       <-- RENAL loss
                                                              bicarbonate owns
                                                              f_ion

                      - CLhd * ACID                        <-- DIALYSIS
                                                              haemodialysis owns
                                                              this term alone

                      - Vmax_THF * ACID/(Km_THF + ACID)    <-- FOLATE-dependent
                                                              oxidation to CO2
                                                              (formate only);
                                                              folinic acid owns
                                                              Vmax_THF

     and the DAMAGE is not the plasma integral but the CNS integral, gated by
     a term that nobody prescribes and everybody measures:

       d(ACID_cns)/dt = PS * f_HA(pH_plasma) * (ACID_plasma - ACID_cns/r)
              f_HA(pH) = 1/(1 + 10^(pH - pKa))     pKa(formic)   = 3.75
                                                   pKa(glycolic) = 3.83

     Five statements follow as ARITHMETIC, not as assertions, and the whole
     map is drawn so that each of them can be traced with a finger:

     (1) THE TWO GAPS ARE ONE REACTION READ TWICE, AND THEIR SUM IS THE DOSE.
         One mole of methanol becomes one mole of formate; the proton it
         carries titrates one mole of bicarbonate, which leaves the body as
         CO2.  Sodium does not move.  So the measured osmolality loses exactly
         the particle the methanol contributed (formate replaces the departed
         bicarbonate one-for-one), while the anion gap gains exactly the same
         number of mEq.  OSMOLAL GAP FALLS BY X, ANION GAP RISES BY X.  The
         RATIO of the two gaps is therefore a CLOCK that reads how far the
         reaction has run, and their SUM is a conserved estimate of the dose
         that is independent of when the patient walked in.  A normal osmolal
         gap with a wide anion gap does not mean a mild poisoning; it means a
         LATE one, in which fomepizole has almost nothing left to block.
         Cluster 10 exists to be read together with cluster 9.

     (2) pH BEATS CONCENTRATION AS A PROGNOSTICATOR BECAUSE pH IS IN THE
         DELIVERY TERM, NOT ONLY IN THE INJURY TERM.  Only un-ionised formic
         acid crosses a membrane.  Going from pH 7.40 to 7.00 multiplies the
         permeant fraction by 2.51 and to pH 6.80 by 3.98 -- at UNCHANGED
         total plasma formate.  Two patients with the same formate level and a
         0.5 pH-unit difference are not two similar patients; they differ
         two- to three-fold in the only quantity that reaches the putamen and
         the optic nerve.  The clinical observation that admission pH predicts
         blindness and death better than the methanol level is an OUTPUT of
         the ion-trapping equation in cluster 11, not a fitted correlation --
         and it is also why bicarbonate is mechanistic therapy rather than
         cosmetic number-fixing: it lowers f_HA into the brain and RAISES
         f_ion in the urine, two effects of one equation with the same sign.

     (3) FOMEPIZOLE AND DIALYSIS ARE NOT ALTERNATIVES; THEY OWN DIFFERENT
         TERMS, AND WHICH ONE MATTERS IS DECIDED BY WHICH ALCOHOL IT IS.
         Blocking ADH does not remove one molecule of poison -- it converts a
         self-clearing toxin into a slowly-leaking reservoir.  Methanol's
         half-life goes from about 3 h (metabolic) to 43-52 h (blocked),
         because its renal and pulmonary clearances are near-nil.  Ethylene
         glycol's blocked half-life is only about 17 h, because 20-30% of it
         leaves unchanged in urine.  SAME antidote, SAME mechanism, opposite
         management: ADH blockade can be definitive monotherapy for ethylene
         glycol with intact kidneys, and is a holding action for methanol.
         The difference is one clearance term, drawn explicitly in cluster 2.

     (4) THE ANTIDOTE IS DIALYSED TOO, SO THE TWO THERAPIES INTERFERE.
         Fomepizole is a 82-Da, minimally-bound, 0.6-0.8 L/kg molecule -- an
         ideal dialysis substrate.  A dosing schedule that is not compressed
         during haemodialysis un-blocks ADH at precisely the moment plasma
         parent alcohol is still high, so the acid-formation flux can SPIKE
         mid-session.  Cluster 17 draws this as a loop rather than a footnote.

     (5) ETHYLENE GLYCOL'S TERMINAL LESION IS A CRYSTAL, SO ITS TOXICITY IS
         RATE-DEPENDENT WHERE METHANOL'S IS DOSE-DEPENDENT.  Calcium oxalate
         precipitates above a solubility product; below it, oxalate is simply
         excreted.  Slowing the flux therefore prevents nephrocalcinosis at an
         unchanged total dose -- there is no analogous threshold for formate.
         And because each mole of deposited crystal sequesters one mole of
         calcium, the ionised calcium is a stoichiometric READ-OUT of crystal
         burden, which is why hypocalcaemia and a long QT track renal injury.
         The glyoxylate branch point (cluster 6) is where thiamine and
         pyridoxine act: they change a BRANCH RATIO, not a flux, so their
         benefit vanishes once fomepizole has closed the flux -- a predicted
         non-additivity, drawn as competing edges out of one node.

     Two deliberate CONTROLS are included in cluster 20 because they are what
     make the argument falsifiable at the bedside: isopropanol (osmolal gap,
     ketosis, NO anion gap -- an alcohol whose metabolite is not an acid) and
     propylene glycol (osmolal gap AND anion gap, but the acid is L-lactate
     and the source is the ICU's own lorazepam infusion).
   =========================================================================== */

digraph TAP_QSP {
  graph [rankdir=TB, splines=spline, overlap=false, newrank=true,
         nodesep=0.26, ranksep=0.50, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11,
         compound=true, labelloc="t", fontcolor="#1a1a2e",
         label=<<B>Toxic Alcohol Poisoning (Methanol · Ethylene Glycol) — Quantitative Systems Pharmacology Map</B><BR/>
                <FONT POINT-SIZE="11">the parent alcohol is an osmole, the metabolite is the poison: injury = ∫(ADH flux)dt gated by pH-dependent ion trapping<BR/>
                ADH/ALDH kinetics · competitive blockade (fomepizole · ethanol) · formate–folate disposition · glyoxylate branch ratio · CaOx supersaturation<BR/>
                osmolal-gap ↔ anion-gap clock · extracorporeal removal and antidote co-removal · putaminal / optic-nerve and renal endpoints</FONT>>];
  node  [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9,
         color="#33334d", penwidth=0.8, margin="0.07,0.04"];
  edge  [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=8, color="#5a5a72", arrowsize=0.6, penwidth=0.9];

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- palette
     exposure / source        #efe4d2     parent alcohol (osmole)  #e8e2f7
     enzyme / kinetics        #fde6d8     acid metabolite (poison) #ffd9d9
     folate / branch chemistry#fdf3d0     crystal / precipitate    #e6e0f7
     acid-base                #d7e9ff     gaps / diagnostics       #efefef
     antidote & drug PK       #d9f2e6     extracorporeal           #dfeef7
     organ injury             #ffdcc7     clinical endpoints       #cfe4ff
     mitochondrial / energy   #ffe9c7     controls / mimics        #e2f0e2
     ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /* =====================================================================
     1 · EXPOSURE · SOURCE · ROUTE · ABSORPTION
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_exp {
    label=<<B>1 · Exposure · source products · route · absorption</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#faf6ef"; color="#b9a888"; fontsize=10;
    SRCM    [label="Methanol sources\nillicit/adulterated spirits · windshield\nwasher · denatured alcohol · fuel · solvent\nhand-sanitiser substitution (2020)", fillcolor="#efe4d2"];
    SRCE    [label="Ethylene glycol sources\nengine coolant/antifreeze · brake &\nhydraulic fluid · de-icer · deliberate\nself-harm · covert administration", fillcolor="#efe4d2"];
    SRCD    [label="Diethylene glycol (DEG)\ncontaminated syrups/glycerin\n→ HEAA, not glycolate\n(mass paediatric outbreaks)", fillcolor="#efe4d2"];
    SRCP    [label="Propylene glycol\niatrogenic: lorazepam/diazepam,\nphenytoin, TMP-SMX diluent\n→ L-lactate (a MIMIC, not a poison)", fillcolor="#e2f0e2"];
    ROUTPO  [label="Oral ingestion (>95%)\nrapid: Tmax 30–90 min\nfasted absorption near-complete", fillcolor="#efe4d2"];
    ROUTIH  [label="Inhalational / dermal\nsolvent abuse, occupational,\npaediatric sanitiser exposure\n→ slow, low-peak, same metabolite", fillcolor="#efe4d2"];
    KAG     [label="Gastric emptying (KA)\nco-ingested ethanol and food\nSLOW absorption → flatter peak", fillcolor="#efe4d2"];
    DOSEM   [label="Methanol toxic dose\n≥0.3–1.0 g/kg lethal untreated\n~30 mL of 40% v/v adult minimum\nblindness reported at ~0.1 g/kg", fillcolor="#efe4d2"];
    DOSEE   [label="Ethylene glycol toxic dose\n~1.4–1.6 mL/kg lethal untreated\n(≈100 mL of 95% adult)\nchild: a mouthful", fillcolor="#efe4d2"];
    BITTER  [label="Denatonium bitterant / fluorescein\nvoluntary antifreeze additive\ndeters but does not prevent\n(useless in deliberate ingestion)", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    GUTM    [label="GUTM — methanol in gut lumen\nstate variable (depot)", fillcolor="#e8e2f7", penwidth=1.6];
    GUTE    [label="GUTE — ethylene glycol in gut lumen\nstate variable (depot)", fillcolor="#e8e2f7", penwidth=1.6];

    SRCM -> ROUTPO; SRCE -> ROUTPO; SRCD -> ROUTPO; SRCP -> ROUTIH [style=dashed];
    SRCM -> ROUTIH [style=dashed];
    ROUTPO -> KAG; KAG -> GUTM; KAG -> GUTE;
    ROUTIH -> GUTM [style=dashed, label="systemic\nentry"];
    DOSEM -> GUTM [style=dotted]; DOSEE -> GUTE [style=dotted];
    BITTER -> SRCE [style=dotted, label="labelling"];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     2 · PARENT ALCOHOL DISPOSITION — the cluster that splits the two diseases
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_par {
    label=<<B>2 · Parent alcohol disposition — where methanol and ethylene glycol part company</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#f6f4fc"; color="#9e94c4"; fontsize=10;
    MEOHC   [label="MEOHC — methanol, central\nplasma/total-body-water pool\nstate variable", fillcolor="#e8e2f7", penwidth=1.8];
    MEOHP   [label="MEOHP — methanol, peripheral\nslow tissue compartment\n(source of post-dialysis rebound)", fillcolor="#e8e2f7", penwidth=1.4];
    EGC     [label="EGC — ethylene glycol, central\nplasma/TBW pool\nstate variable", fillcolor="#e8e2f7", penwidth=1.8];
    EGP     [label="EGP — ethylene glycol, peripheral\nslow tissue compartment", fillcolor="#e8e2f7", penwidth=1.4];
    VDM     [label="Vd(methanol) ≈ 0.6–0.7 L/kg\n= total body water\nnegligible protein binding", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    VDE     [label="Vd(EG) ≈ 0.5–0.8 L/kg\nnegligible protein binding\nMW 62.07", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    CLRM    [label="Renal CL(methanol) — NEGLIGIBLE\n<3–5% of dose unchanged\n(freely filtered, then reabsorbed\nwith water)", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];
    CLPM    [label="Pulmonary CL(methanol) — small\nblood:air ≈ 1350:1\nminutes of exhalation ≠ a route out", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    CLRE    [label="Renal CL(EG) — SUBSTANTIAL\n20–30% excreted unchanged;\nGFR-proportional\n→ the term that changes management", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.6];
    HLTAB   [label="Half-life bookkeeping\nMETHANOL  metabolic ~3 h (low C) →\n  ADH-blocked 43–52 h\nEG        metabolic ~3–5 h →\n  ADH-blocked ~17 h (kidneys intact)\nHD (either) 1–3 h", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.4];
    ZEROO   [label="Capacity-limited elimination\nmethanol falls ~8.5 mg/dL/h\n(range 4.4–25) at saturating C\n→ apparent zero order, not first", fillcolor="#fde6d8"];

    GUTM -> MEOHC [label="ka"]; GUTE -> EGC [label="ka"];
    MEOHC -> MEOHP [dir=both, label="k12/k21"];
    EGC   -> EGP   [dir=both, label="k12/k21"];
    VDM -> MEOHC [style=dotted]; VDE -> EGC [style=dotted];
    MEOHC -> CLRM; MEOHC -> CLPM; EGC -> CLRE;
    ZEROO -> MEOHC [style=dotted, label="Vmax/Km"];
    HLTAB -> CLRE [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     3 · ADH / ALDH OXIDATION CASCADE — the only source of toxicity
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_adh {
    label=<<B>3 · Hepatic ADH → ALDH oxidation cascade — the flux that IS the disease</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fdf1ea"; color="#d0a288"; fontsize=10;
    ADH     [label="ADH1B/ADH1C (cytosolic, hepatic)\nRATE-LIMITING step for both alcohols\nKm: ethanol ~0.5–2 mM ≪ EG ~1–3 mM\n     ≪ methanol ~7–10 mM", fillcolor="#fde6d8", penwidth=1.8];
    NADR    [label="NAD⁺/NADH redox couple\nADH consumes NAD⁺; ethanol load\nraises NADH → lactate/pyruvate ↑\n(a co-ingestion artefact)", fillcolor="#fde6d8"];
    FALD    [label="Formaldehyde\nt½ ≈ 1–2 min — never measurable\nnot the systemic toxin\n(local carbonyl chemistry only)", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];
    ALDH    [label="ALDH2 (mitochondrial) — very fast\nformaldehyde → formate\nglycolaldehyde → glycolate\nnever rate-limiting", fillcolor="#fde6d8"];
    GALD    [label="Glycolaldehyde\nmost directly cytotoxic EG metabolite\n(proximal tubular epithelium)\nt½ minutes", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];
    CATM    [label="Catalase / CYP2E1 minor routes\n<10% of methanol oxidation in man\n(the reason rodent extrapolation fails)", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    FLUXA   [label="FLUX_ACID = Vmax·P / (Km·INH + P)\nthe single expression every therapy\ncompetes for\nstate-driving rate, not a state", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.8, shape=box3d];

    MEOHC -> ADH [label="substrate"]; EGC -> ADH [label="substrate"];
    ADH -> FALD [label="methanol\nbranch"]; ADH -> GALD [label="EG\nbranch"];
    FALD -> ALDH; GALD -> ALDH;
    NADR -> ADH [dir=both, label="cofactor"];
    CATM -> FALD [style=dashed];
    ADH -> FLUXA [style=bold]; ALDH -> FLUXA [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     4 · COMPETITIVE BLOCKADE — fomepizole vs ethanol, as arithmetic
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_inh {
    label=<<B>4 · Competitive ADH blockade — the denominator that fomepizole and ethanol fight over</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#eef9f3"; color="#7fbd9e"; fontsize=10;
    FOMC    [label="FOMC — fomepizole (4-methylpyrazole)\nplasma concentration\nstate variable", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.8];
    FOMKI   [label="Fomepizole Ki ≈ 0.1–0.5 µM\nvs methanol Km ~10 mM\n→ 10⁴–10⁵-fold selectivity;\ntherapeutic ~10 µg/mL ≈ 122 µM", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.4];
    ETOHC   [label="ETOHC — ethanol\nplasma concentration\nstate variable (antidote OR co-ingestant)", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.8];
    ETOHKE  [label="Ethanol Ki(app) ≈ 2 mM ≈ 9 mg/dL\ntarget 100–150 mg/dL\n→ inhibition factor ~12–25", fillcolor="#d9f2e6"];
    INHF    [label="INH = 1 + FOMC/Ki + ETOHC/Ke\nthe inhibition factor\n(multiplies Km, not Vmax —\ncompetitive, therefore surmountable)", fillcolor="#fde6d8", penwidth=1.8, shape=box3d];
    BLKCMP  [label="Completeness of blockade, computed\nethanol 100 mg/dL  → INH ≈ 13\nfomepizole 10 µg/mL → INH ≈ 10³\n→ fomepizole is ~2 orders of magnitude\nthe more complete block", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.4];
    SURM    [label="Surmountability\nas P rises, P swamps Km·INH;\nblockade is never absolute at\nenormous ingested doses", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];
    AUTOI   [label="Fomepizole autoinduction\nCL rises after 30–48 h\n→ dose escalation 15 mg/kg q12h", fillcolor="#d9f2e6"];

    FOMC -> INHF [label="/Ki", style=bold]; ETOHC -> INHF [label="/Ke", style=bold];
    FOMKI -> FOMC [style=dotted]; ETOHKE -> ETOHC [style=dotted];
    INHF -> ADH [label="Km × INH", style=bold, color="#2e7d5b", penwidth=1.6];
    INHF -> FLUXA [label="denominator", style=bold, color="#2e7d5b"];
    FOMKI -> BLKCMP [style=dotted]; ETOHKE -> BLKCMP [style=dotted];
    SURM -> INHF [style=dashed, label="P ≫ Km·INH"];
    AUTOI -> FOMC [label="CL ↑"];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     5 · FORMATE DISPOSITION — the folate-dependent exit
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_form {
    label=<<B>5 · Formate disposition — folate-dependent oxidation is the only real exit</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fdfaee"; color="#c7b878"; fontsize=10;
    FORMC   [label="FORMC — plasma formate\nTHE methanol toxin\nstate variable", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.8];
    FORMP   [label="FORMP — peripheral formate\ntissue pool", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.4];
    FTHFS   [label="10-formyl-THF synthetase\n(FTHFS/MTHFD1L pathway)\nformate + THF → 10-CHO-THF → CO₂\nsaturable, folate-dependent", fillcolor="#fdf3d0", penwidth=1.6];
    THFP    [label="THFP — hepatic tetrahydrofolate pool\nstate variable;\nHUMAN/PRIMATE stores are LOW\n→ formate accumulates", fillcolor="#fdf3d0", penwidth=1.6];
    SPEC    [label="Species divergence\nrat: high folate → no accumulation,\nno acidosis, no blindness\n→ rodent studies mislead", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    FOLIN   [label="Folinic acid (leucovorin) 1 mg/kg q4–6 h\n(or folic acid) — raises Vmax_THF\nbenefit modest vs dialysis; low harm", fillcolor="#d9f2e6"];
    FORMR   [label="Renal formate excretion\npH-dependent: ionised HCOO⁻ trapped\nin alkaline urine → CL ↑\n(the second half of the bicarb effect)", fillcolor="#d7e9ff", penwidth=1.4];
    FORMCO2 [label="Formate → CO₂ + H₂O\nterminal, non-toxic exit", fillcolor="#e2f0e2"];
    ALCFOL  [label="Chronic alcohol use / malnutrition\n→ folate deplete → Vmax_THF ↓\n→ same dose, worse outcome", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];

    ALDH -> FORMC [label="formaldehyde\n→ formate", style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    FLUXA -> FORMC [style=bold, color="#b03a3a"];
    FORMC -> FORMP [dir=both, label="k13/k31"];
    FORMC -> FTHFS -> FORMCO2;
    THFP -> FTHFS [label="cofactor", style=bold];
    FOLIN -> THFP [label="repletes"]; SPEC -> THFP [style=dotted];
    ALCFOL -> THFP [label="depletes", color="#b03a3a"];
    FORMC -> FORMR;
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     6 · GLYOXYLATE BRANCH POINT — where thiamine and pyridoxine act
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_glyox {
    label=<<B>6 · Glycolate → glyoxylate branch ratio — thiamine and pyridoxine change a RATIO, not a flux</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fdfaee"; color="#c7b878"; fontsize=10;
    GLYC    [label="GLYC — plasma glycolate\nTHE acidosis of ethylene glycol\n(correlates with pH and outcome\nbetter than EG itself)", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.8];
    GLYCP   [label="GLYCP — peripheral glycolate", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.4];
    GLX     [label="Glyoxylate — the branch node\nshort-lived, itself an ox-phos inhibitor", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.6];
    OXBR    [label="LDH / glycolate oxidase branch\nglyoxylate → OXALATE\nthe nephrotoxic route", fillcolor="#e6e0f7", penwidth=1.6];
    THBR    [label="AST branch (thiamine-dependent)\nglyoxylate → α-hydroxy-β-ketoadipate\nharmless", fillcolor="#fdf3d0"];
    PYBR    [label="ALT branch (pyridoxine-dependent)\nglyoxylate → GLYCINE → hippurate\nharmless", fillcolor="#fdf3d0"];
    THIA    [label="Thiamine 100 mg IV\nraises AST-branch capacity\n(cheap, no harm, human evidence weak)", fillcolor="#d9f2e6"];
    PYRI    [label="Pyridoxine 50–100 mg IV\nraises ALT-branch capacity", fillcolor="#d9f2e6"];
    NONADD  [label="PREDICTED NON-ADDITIVITY\nbranch cofactors matter only while\nflux is high; once ADH is blocked\nthere is no glyoxylate to redirect", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.6];
    GLYCR   [label="Renal glycolate excretion\npH-dependent, GFR-dependent\n(falls as AKI develops → self-amplifying)", fillcolor="#d7e9ff"];
    EGFORM  [label="Minor EG → formate route\n(small; why some EG cases show\nmixed metabolite profiles)", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];

    ALDH -> GLYC [label="glycolaldehyde\n→ glycolate", style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    FLUXA -> GLYC [style=bold, color="#b03a3a"];
    GLYC -> GLYCP [dir=both]; GLYC -> GLX [label="glycolate\noxidase"];
    GLX -> OXBR [label="~2/3 in\nuntreated"];
    GLX -> THBR [label="thiamine-\ndependent"]; GLX -> PYBR [label="pyridoxine-\ndependent"];
    THIA -> THBR [label="Vmax ↑"]; PYRI -> PYBR [label="Vmax ↑"];
    NONADD -> THBR [style=dotted]; NONADD -> PYBR [style=dotted];
    GLYC -> GLYCR; GLYC -> EGFORM [style=dashed];
    EGFORM -> FORMC [style=dashed];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     7 · CALCIUM OXALATE — a THRESHOLD, therefore a rate-dependent toxicity
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_caox {
    label=<<B>7 · Calcium oxalate supersaturation — the only threshold in the model</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#f4f1fb"; color="#a396cc"; fontsize=10;
    OXAL    [label="OXAL — plasma oxalate\nstate variable\nMW 90; solubility-limited", fillcolor="#e6e0f7", penwidth=1.8];
    SSPROD  [label="Supersaturation = [Ca²⁺][Ox²⁻]/Ksp\nno crystal below 1.0,\nrunaway nucleation above it\n→ TOXICITY IS RATE-DEPENDENT", fillcolor="#e6e0f7", penwidth=1.8, shape=box3d];
    NUCL    [label="Nucleation + crystal growth\ntubular fluid concentrates 50–100×\n→ medulla nucleates first", fillcolor="#e6e0f7"];
    CAOXK   [label="CAOXK — renal CaOx burden\nstate variable\n(monohydrate 'envelope' crystals)", fillcolor="#e6e0f7", penwidth=1.6];
    CAOXT   [label="CAOXT — extrarenal CaOx deposition\nbrain, myocardium, vessel walls,\nretina (autopsy finding)", fillcolor="#e6e0f7", penwidth=1.4];
    CASTOI  [label="STOICHIOMETRIC SHADOW\n1 mmol CaOx deposited = 1 mmol Ca²⁺\nremoved → ionised calcium is a\nREAD-OUT of crystal burden", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.6];
    CAION   [label="CAION — ionised calcium\nstate variable", fillcolor="#ffdcc7", penwidth=1.6];
    URXTAL  [label="Urinary CaOx crystals\ninsensitive & late; monohydrate\nneedles mis-read as hippurate", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    UFLUOR  [label="Urine fluorescence under Wood's lamp\npoor sensitivity/specificity\n— do not rule out on it", fillcolor="#efefef"];

    OXBR -> OXAL [style=bold, color="#5b4a99", penwidth=1.6];
    OXAL -> SSPROD; CAION -> SSPROD [label="[Ca²⁺]"];
    SSPROD -> NUCL [label="> 1.0", style=bold];
    NUCL -> CAOXK [style=bold]; NUCL -> CAOXT;
    CAOXK -> CASTOI; CAOXT -> CASTOI;
    CASTOI -> CAION [label="consumes", color="#b03a3a", style=bold];
    CAOXK -> URXTAL [style=dotted]; URXTAL -> UFLUOR [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     8 · MITOCHONDRIAL / ENERGETIC TOXICITY
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_mito {
    label=<<B>8 · Mitochondrial toxicity — formate at cytochrome c oxidase, and the lactate it manufactures</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fff8ec"; color="#d3b273"; fontsize=10;
    CCO     [label="Cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV)\nformate binds ferric haem a₃\nKi ~5–30 mM — WEAK, so only high\nlocal (CNS) formate matters", fillcolor="#ffe9c7", penwidth=1.8];
    ATPC    [label="ATPC — CNS ATP / energy charge\nstate variable", fillcolor="#ffe9c7", penwidth=1.6];
    HISTOX  [label="Histotoxic hypoxia\nnormal PaO₂, normal SaO₂,\nfailing oxidative phosphorylation\n(cyanide-like, milder)", fillcolor="#ffe9c7"];
    LACT    [label="LACT — plasma L-lactate\nstate variable\n(real, from anaerobiosis + NADH)", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.6];
    ROSN    [label="ROS / lipid peroxidation\nGSH depletion (retina, tubule)", fillcolor="#ffe9c7"];
    LGAP    [label="'LACTATE GAP' (diagnostic)\nglycolate cross-reacts on some\nblood-gas lactate oxidase electrodes\n→ POC lactate ≫ laboratory lactate", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.6];
    CREATI  [label="Spurious creatinine rise\nglycolate interferes with Jaffe assay\n→ 'AKI' before any AKI", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    GLXMITO [label="Glyoxylate + glycolaldehyde\ndirect ox-phos and Krebs inhibition\n(the EG parallel of formate)", fillcolor="#ffe9c7"];

    FORMC -> CCO [label="f_HA-gated\ndelivery", style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    CCO -> ATPC [label="ATP ↓", color="#b03a3a"];
    CCO -> HISTOX; HISTOX -> LACT [style=bold];
    ATPC -> LACT [label="anaerobic\nglycolysis"];
    NADR -> LACT [style=dashed, label="NADH ↑"];
    CCO -> ROSN; GLX -> GLXMITO -> ATPC;
    GALD -> GLXMITO [style=dashed];
    GLYC -> LGAP [style=dotted, label="assay\ninterference"];
    GLYC -> CREATI [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     9 · ACID–BASE — bicarbonate as a titrated quantity
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_ab {
    label=<<B>9 · Acid–base — the anion gap is the acid metabolite, mole for mole</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#eef5ff"; color="#8fb0d8"; fontsize=10;
    HLOAD   [label="Titratable acid load (mmol/L)\n= formate + glycolate + lactate\n(+ ketones if starved/alcoholic)", fillcolor="#d7e9ff", penwidth=1.6];
    HCO3    [label="HCO3 — plasma bicarbonate\nstate variable\nconsumed 1:1 by the acid load", fillcolor="#d7e9ff", penwidth=1.8];
    PHB     [label="pH — arterial\nHenderson–Hasselbalch from\nHCO3 and PaCO₂\nstate-derived output", fillcolor="#d7e9ff", penwidth=1.8];
    PACO2   [label="PACO2 — arterial PaCO₂\nstate variable with a LAG\nKussmaul respiration; Winter's\nexpected 1.5·HCO3 + 8", fillcolor="#d7e9ff", penwidth=1.6];
    BUFT    [label="Tissue / bone buffering\nnon-bicarbonate buffer capacity\n(why HCO3 falls less than the acid load)", fillcolor="#d7e9ff"];
    AGAP    [label="AG — anion gap = Na⁺ − Cl⁻ − HCO3⁻\nRISES exactly as much as HCO3 falls\n(the metabolite IS the unmeasured anion)", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.8];
    BICRX   [label="Sodium bicarbonate therapy\n1–2 mEq/kg then infusion,\ntarget pH > 7.30\nlarge doses often needed", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.6];
    BICCA   [label="Alkalinisation lowers ionised Ca²⁺\n→ tetany risk; in EG it also raises\nCaOx supersaturation transiently", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];
    RESPF   [label="Respiratory failure / obtundation\nloss of compensation → pH collapses\n(the terminal step in most deaths)", fillcolor="#ffdcc7"];

    FORMC -> HLOAD [style=bold]; GLYC -> HLOAD [style=bold]; LACT -> HLOAD;
    HLOAD -> HCO3 [label="1:1 titration", style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    HLOAD -> AGAP [label="unmeasured\nanion", style=bold];
    HCO3 -> PHB; PACO2 -> PHB; BUFT -> HCO3 [style=dashed];
    PHB -> PACO2 [label="central\nchemoreceptor", style=dashed];
    BICRX -> HCO3 [label="adds", color="#2e7d5b", style=bold];
    BICRX -> BICCA; BICCA -> CAION [color="#b03a3a"];
    PACO2 -> RESPF [style=dashed]; RESPF -> PHB [color="#b03a3a", label="compensation\nlost"];
    HCO3 -> AGAP [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     10 · THE TWO-GAP CLOCK — the diagnostic engine of the whole model
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_gap {
    label=<<B>10 · The osmolal-gap ↔ anion-gap clock — one reaction, two read-outs, conserved sum</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#f4f4f4"; color="#9a9a9a"; fontsize=10;
    OSMM    [label="Measured osmolality\nFREEZING-POINT DEPRESSION ONLY.\nVapour-pressure osmometry boils off\nthe volatile alcohol → gap missed", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.6];
    OSMC    [label="Calculated osmolarity\n2·Na + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8\n(ethanol/2.17 must be added if present)", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    OGAP    [label="OG — osmolal gap = measured − calculated\nfalls as the reaction runs\nstate-derived output", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.8];
    CONVM   [label="Methanol: 1 mg/dL = 0.312 mOsm/kg\n(MW 32.04)", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    CONVE   [label="Ethylene glycol: 1 mg/dL = 0.161 mOsm/kg\n(MW 62.07)", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    CLOCK   [label="THE CLOCK\nd(OG)/dt = −flux ; d(AG)/dt = +flux\nOG/AG ratio reads TIME SINCE INGESTION,\nnot severity", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.8, shape=box3d];
    INVAR   [label="THE INVARIANT\nOG + AG ≈ total ingested mmol/L,\nconserved until renal/dialytic loss\n→ a dose estimate independent of\nwhen the patient presented", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.8, shape=box3d];
    PITF1   [label="PITFALL — normal OG is NOT reassuring\nlate presenter: OG normal, AG wide,\nfomepizole has little left to block\n→ dialysis, not antidote, is the therapy", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.6];
    PITF2   [label="PITFALL — baseline OG is unknown\npopulation baseline spans −14 to +10\n→ a 'gap of 10' is uninterpretable\nin an individual", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];
    PITF3   [label="PITFALL — ethanol contribution\nco-ingested ethanol inflates OG\nand must be subtracted before the\nOG is attributed to methanol", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];
    GCASSAY [label="Definitive assay: GC / GC-MS\nturnaround hours–days, few centres\n→ decisions are made on gaps", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.4];

    MEOHC -> OGAP [label="× 0.312", style=bold]; EGC -> OGAP [label="× 0.161", style=bold];
    ETOHC -> OGAP [label="× 0.462\n(must subtract)", style=dashed];
    OSMM -> OGAP; OSMC -> OGAP;
    CONVM -> OGAP [style=dotted]; CONVE -> OGAP [style=dotted];
    OGAP -> CLOCK [style=bold]; AGAP -> CLOCK [style=bold];
    CLOCK -> INVAR [style=bold];
    FLUXA -> CLOCK [label="the shared\nflux", style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    CLOCK -> PITF1; PITF2 -> OGAP [style=dashed]; PITF3 -> OGAP [style=dashed];
    GCASSAY -> MEOHC [style=dotted]; GCASSAY -> EGC [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     11 · ION TRAPPING — the mechanistic heart of prognosis
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_trap {
    label=<<B>11 · pH-dependent ion trapping — why pH outranks concentration as a prognosticator</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fff0f0"; color="#cc8f8f"; fontsize=10;
    PKAF    [label="Formic acid pKa = 3.75\nGlycolic acid pKa = 3.83\nboth ~100% ionised at plasma pH", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];
    FHA     [label="f_HA(pH) = 1/(1 + 10^(pH − pKa))\npH 7.40 → 2.24e−4\npH 7.20 → 3.55e−4  (×1.59)\npH 7.00 → 5.62e−4  (×2.51)\npH 6.80 → 8.91e−4  (×3.98)", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.8, shape=box3d];
    PSBBB   [label="PS·f_HA — effective permeability×area\nonly the un-ionised acid diffuses;\nthe anion is excluded", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.6];
    FORMCNS [label="FORMCNS — CNS/putaminal formate\nstate variable\nTHE quantity that injures", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.8];
    FORMVIT [label="FORMVIT — vitreous / retinal formate\nstate variable\n(measurably higher than plasma\nin fatal cases)", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.8];
    SELFAMP [label="SELF-AMPLIFYING LOOP\nacid load → pH ↓ → f_HA ↑ →\nmore CNS delivery → more ATP failure →\nmore lactate → pH ↓\n(a positive feedback, drawn as one)", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.8];
    URTRAP  [label="Urinary trapping (same equation,\nopposite direction)\nalkaline urine → ionised → not\nreabsorbed → renal CL ↑", fillcolor="#d7e9ff", penwidth=1.4];
    WHYPH   [label="DERIVED CLAIM\nadmission pH predicts blindness/death\nbetter than the methanol level because\npH is in the DELIVERY term, not just\nthe injury term", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.8];

    PKAF -> FHA [style=dotted]; PHB -> FHA [label="pH", style=bold, color="#2b6ca3", penwidth=1.6];
    FHA -> PSBBB [style=bold];
    FORMC -> PSBBB [label="plasma\nformate", style=bold];
    PSBBB -> FORMCNS [style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    PSBBB -> FORMVIT [style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    FORMCNS -> CCO [style=bold, color="#b03a3a"];
    LACT -> SELFAMP; SELFAMP -> PHB [color="#b03a3a"]; FHA -> SELFAMP;
    FHA -> URTRAP; URTRAP -> FORMR [style=bold, color="#2e7d5b"];
    BICRX -> FHA [label="raises pH →\nf_HA ↓", style=bold, color="#2e7d5b", penwidth=1.6];
    FHA -> WHYPH [style=dotted]; PSBBB -> WHYPH [style=dotted];
    GLYC -> PSBBB [style=dashed, label="same equation,\npKa 3.83"];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     12 · CNS INJURY — putamen and optic nerve
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_cns {
    label=<<B>12 · CNS injury — bilateral putaminal necrosis and retrobulbar optic neuropathy</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fff3ec"; color="#d2a58a"; fontsize=10;
    PUTAN   [label="Putamen — why there?\nhighest regional metabolic rate +\nwatershed perfusion + poor\nformate-oxidising capacity", fillcolor="#ffdcc7", penwidth=1.6];
    PUT     [label="PUT — putaminal injury index\nstate variable (integral of CNS\nformate above a threshold)", fillcolor="#ffdcc7", penwidth=1.8];
    HEMOR   [label="Haemorrhagic transformation\nputaminal haemorrhage;\nanticoagulation during HD worsens it", fillcolor="#ffdcc7"];
    OEDEM   [label="Cerebral oedema\ncytotoxic + vasogenic;\nherniation is a mode of death", fillcolor="#ffdcc7"];
    OPTN    [label="Retrobulbar optic nerve\naxonal swelling, demyelination,\noptic disc hyperaemia then pallor", fillcolor="#ffdcc7", penwidth=1.6];
    MULLER  [label="Retinal Müller cells\nlack formate-oxidising capacity;\nATP failure → photoreceptor\nand axonal transport arrest", fillcolor="#ffdcc7"];
    OPTIC   [label="OPTIC — optic injury index\nstate variable (integral of vitreous\nformate above threshold)", fillcolor="#ffdcc7", penwidth=1.8];
    VISUAL  [label="VISUAL — visual acuity (logMAR)\nmodel output;\nsnowfield vision → central scotoma →\nno light perception", fillcolor="#cfe4ff", penwidth=1.8];
    CNSDEP  [label="CNS depression\nEG: 'drunk without the smell';\nmethanol: mild until acidosis\n→ the latent interval", fillcolor="#ffdcc7"];
    MRIT2   [label="MRI: bilateral putaminal T2/FLAIR\nhyperintensity, ± subcortical white\nmatter; strongly prognostic", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    PARKD   [label="Delayed parkinsonism / dystonia\nweeks–months after recovery\n(the surviving putamen lesion)", fillcolor="#cfe4ff"];

    FORMCNS -> PUTAN -> PUT [style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    ATPC -> PUT [style=bold]; ATPC -> OPTIC [style=bold];
    PUT -> HEMOR; PUT -> OEDEM; PUT -> MRIT2 [style=dotted]; PUT -> PARKD;
    FORMVIT -> MULLER -> OPTIC [style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    FORMVIT -> OPTN -> OPTIC [style=bold];
    OPTIC -> VISUAL [style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    MEOHC -> CNSDEP [style=dashed]; EGC -> CNSDEP [style=dashed];
    PHB -> CNSDEP [style=dashed, label="acidosis\ndeepens it"];
    OEDEM -> CNSDEP;
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     13 · RENAL INJURY
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_ren {
    label=<<B>13 · Renal injury — glycolaldehyde plus crystal, and the clearance it destroys</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fff3ec"; color="#d2a58a"; fontsize=10;
    PTINJ   [label="Proximal tubular epithelial injury\nglycolaldehyde + glyoxylate direct\ntoxicity + intratubular crystal\n(two mechanisms, one cell)", fillcolor="#ffdcc7", penwidth=1.6];
    GFR     [label="GFR — glomerular filtration rate\nstate variable\n(both an outcome AND a clearance term)", fillcolor="#ffdcc7", penwidth=1.8];
    AKIS    [label="AKI staging / oliguria\npeak creatinine at 24–72 h;\ndialysis-requiring in severe EG", fillcolor="#cfe4ff"];
    VICIOUS [label="VICIOUS CIRCLE (drawn as a loop)\nAKI → glycolate & EG clearance ↓ →\nmore acid, more oxalate → more AKI", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.8];
    CKDL    [label="Long-term CKD / nephrocalcinosis\nmost recover fully; a minority\nkeep reduced eGFR", fillcolor="#cfe4ff"];
    PREEX   [label="Pre-existing CKD\nlower CLRE and lower oxalate clearance\n→ EG behaves 'like methanol'\n(ADH blockade alone insufficient)", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.4];

    GALD -> PTINJ [style=bold, color="#b03a3a"]; GLX -> PTINJ;
    CAOXK -> PTINJ [style=bold, color="#5b4a99"];
    PTINJ -> GFR [label="↓", color="#b03a3a", style=bold, penwidth=1.6];
    GFR -> AKIS [style=dotted]; GFR -> CKDL [style=dotted];
    GFR -> CLRE [label="proportional", style=bold, color="#2e7d5b", penwidth=1.6];
    GFR -> GLYCR [style=bold]; GFR -> FORMR;
    VICIOUS -> GFR [style=dashed]; GFR -> VICIOUS [style=dashed];
    PREEX -> GFR [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     14 · CALCIUM AND THE HEART
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_ca {
    label=<<B>14 · Calcium and cardiac consequences</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fff3ec"; color="#d2a58a"; fontsize=10;
    QTC     [label="QTc prolongation\nfrom ionised hypocalcaemia\n→ risk of malignant arrhythmia", fillcolor="#cfe4ff", penwidth=1.4];
    TETANY  [label="Tetany / Trousseau / seizures\nlate EG feature", fillcolor="#ffdcc7"];
    CARX    [label="Calcium replacement — CONDITIONAL\ngive for symptomatic/ECG hypocalcaemia;\nempiric loading raises [Ca][Ox]\n→ more crystal", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.4];
    MYOCA   [label="Myocardial CaOx deposition\nmyocarditis-like injury,\nheart failure in massive ingestion", fillcolor="#ffdcc7"];

    CAION -> QTC [style=bold]; CAION -> TETANY;
    CARX -> CAION [color="#2e7d5b"]; CARX -> SSPROD [label="raises\n[Ca²⁺]", color="#b03a3a", style=dashed];
    CAOXT -> MYOCA;
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     15 · FOMEPIZOLE PHARMACOLOGY
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_fom {
    label=<<B>15 · Fomepizole — dosing, PK, and the loop with dialysis</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#eef9f3"; color="#7fbd9e"; fontsize=10;
    FOMD    [label="Fomepizole regimen\n15 mg/kg load, then 10 mg/kg q12h ×4,\nthen 15 mg/kg q12h\nIV or PO (near-complete bioavailability)", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.6];
    FOMPK   [label="Fomepizole PK\nMW 82.1 · Vd 0.6–0.8 L/kg ·\nnegligible binding · Michaelis–Menten\nelimination at therapeutic C", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.6];
    FOMHD   [label="Dosing DURING dialysis\nq4h, or continuous 1.0–1.5 mg/kg/h\n(because HD removes it)", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.6];
    FOMAE   [label="Adverse effects\nheadache, nausea, dizziness,\ninfusion-site irritation,\nmild transaminase rise", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    FOMSUP  [label="Supply and cost\nstockout-prone, expensive;\nin outbreaks it is RATIONED\n→ ethanol becomes the real antidote", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.4];
    FOMSTOP [label="Stopping rule\nstop when the parent alcohol is\n<20 mg/dL AND the patient is\nasymptomatic with normal pH", fillcolor="#d9f2e6"];

    FOMD -> FOMC [label="input", style=bold]; FOMPK -> FOMC [style=dotted];
    FOMHD -> FOMC [style=bold, color="#2e7d5b"]; FOMC -> FOMAE [style=dotted];
    FOMSUP -> FOMD [style=dashed]; FOMSTOP -> FOMC [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     16 · ETHANOL AS ANTIDOTE
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_eth {
    label=<<B>16 · Ethanol as antidote — why it works, and why it loses</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#eef9f3"; color="#7fbd9e"; fontsize=10;
    ETHD    [label="Ethanol regimen\n0.6–0.8 g/kg load (10% IV or PO spirit),\nthen 66–154 mg/kg/h;\ntarget 100–150 mg/dL", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.6];
    ETHZO   [label="Zero-order ethanol elimination\n→ concentration is unstable;\nhourly levels needed; wide inter-\nindividual Vmax (chronic drinkers)", fillcolor="#fde6d8", penwidth=1.4];
    ETHAE   [label="Adverse effects\nCNS depression on top of the poison,\nhypoglycaemia (esp. children),\nphlebitis, added osmolal load", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];
    ETHHD   [label="Ethanol during dialysis\ncleared rapidly → maintenance dose\n2–3× higher, or add ethanol to\nthe dialysate", fillcolor="#d9f2e6"];
    ETHCO   [label="Co-ingested ethanol (not therapy)\nboth PROTECTS and DELAYS:\npatients present late, on day 2,\nwith the reaction only then running", fillcolor="#fde6d8", penwidth=1.6];
    ETHLAT  [label="THE LATENT INTERVAL IS AN OUTPUT\npeak acid lags peak alcohol;\nco-ingested ethanol lengthens the lag →\nthe classic 12–24 h asymptomatic window", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.6];

    ETHD -> ETOHC [label="input", style=bold]; ETHZO -> ETOHC [style=dotted];
    ETOHC -> ETHAE [style=dotted]; ETHHD -> ETOHC [style=bold];
    ETHCO -> ETOHC [style=bold]; ETHCO -> ETHLAT [style=dotted];
    ETOHC -> ETHLAT [style=dotted]; FLUXA -> ETHLAT [style=dotted];
    ETHAE -> CNSDEP [color="#b03a3a", style=dashed];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     17 · EXTRACORPOREAL REMOVAL — and the antidote it also removes
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_hd {
    label=<<B>17 · Extracorporeal removal — the only term that empties the reservoir</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#eef5fb"; color="#8aa9c4"; fontsize=10;
    IHD     [label="Intermittent haemodialysis\nCL 200–300 mL/min for methanol,\nglycolate, formate, EG\n(small, unbound, low Vd = ideal)", fillcolor="#dfeef7", penwidth=1.8];
    CRRT    [label="CVVHD/CVVHDF\nCL only 30–50 mL/min\n→ 5–10× slower; acceptable only\nif IHD is unavailable, and for longer", fillcolor="#dfeef7", penwidth=1.4];
    HDCRIT  [label="Indications (EXTRIP-style)\npH <7.15–7.20 · AG >24 · new visual or\nCNS deficit · AKI · methanol >70 mg/dL\n(>50 if no fomepizole)", fillcolor="#dfeef7", penwidth=1.6];
    HDEND   [label="Stopping rule\nAG normalised, pH >7.30, parent\nalcohol <20 mg/dL, symptoms resolved\n(NOT a fixed number of hours)", fillcolor="#dfeef7"];
    REBND   [label="POST-DIALYSIS REBOUND\nperipheral compartment re-equilibrates;\nplasma rises again for hours\n→ measure AFTER, not during", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.6];
    HDBIC   [label="Dialysate bicarbonate\ncorrects the acidosis in the same\nsession → f_HA falls → CNS delivery\nfalls. One machine, two terms.", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.6];
    HDANT   [label="THE LOOP: the antidote is dialysed too\nfomepizole (82 Da) and ethanol are\nremoved as efficiently as the poison →\nun-blocked ADH while parent alcohol\nis still high → FLUX SPIKE mid-session", fillcolor="#ffd9d9", penwidth=1.8, shape=box3d];
    HDHEP   [label="Anticoagulation for the circuit\n→ risk in putaminal haemorrhage;\nregional citrate as an alternative", fillcolor="#ffd9d9"];

    HDCRIT -> IHD [style=dotted]; HDCRIT -> CRRT [style=dotted];
    IHD -> MEOHC [label="CLhd", style=bold, color="#2b6ca3", penwidth=1.6];
    IHD -> EGC [label="CLhd", style=bold, color="#2b6ca3", penwidth=1.6];
    IHD -> FORMC [label="CLhd", style=bold, color="#2b6ca3", penwidth=1.6];
    IHD -> GLYC [label="CLhd", style=bold, color="#2b6ca3", penwidth=1.6];
    IHD -> FOMC [label="removes\nthe antidote", style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    IHD -> ETOHC [label="removes", style=bold, color="#b03a3a"];
    CRRT -> MEOHC [style=dashed]; CRRT -> GLYC [style=dashed];
    IHD -> HDBIC; HDBIC -> HCO3 [style=bold, color="#2e7d5b"];
    MEOHP -> REBND [style=bold]; GLYCP -> REBND; REBND -> MEOHC [color="#b03a3a"];
    HDANT -> FLUXA [label="flux spike", style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    IHD -> HDANT [style=bold]; FOMHD -> HDANT [label="prevented by\nq4h dosing", style=dashed, color="#2e7d5b"];
    IHD -> HDHEP [style=dotted]; HDHEP -> HEMOR [style=dashed, color="#b03a3a"];
    IHD -> HDEND [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     18 · SUPPORTIVE AND ADJUNCTIVE CARE
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_sup {
    label=<<B>18 · Supportive and adjunctive care</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#eef9f3"; color="#7fbd9e"; fontsize=10;
    FLUID   [label="Volume resuscitation\nrestores GFR-dependent clearance\n(the one supportive measure with a\nquantifiable place in the equations)", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.4];
    AIRWAY  [label="Airway / ventilation\nprotects the respiratory compensation\nthat is holding the pH up", fillcolor="#d9f2e6", penwidth=1.4];
    NOGID   [label="GI decontamination — NO role\nalcohols are absorbed too fast;\ncharcoal does not bind them", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    NOLIPID [label="Lipid emulsion — NO role\n(these are hydrophilic poisons)", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    DEGSPEC [label="Diethylene glycol — a different disease\nmetabolite is HEAA, not glycolate;\nADH blockade is plausible but unproven;\nneuropathy + AKI dominate", fillcolor="#e2f0e2", penwidth=1.4];
    OUTBRK  [label="Mass-poisoning logistics\noutbreaks (Estonia 2001, Libya 2013,\nIran 2020, India, Indonesia):\ntriage on gaps, ration fomepizole,\nethanol + dialysis carry the load", fillcolor="#cfe4ff", penwidth=1.4];

    FLUID -> GFR [label="restores", color="#2e7d5b", style=bold];
    AIRWAY -> PACO2 [color="#2e7d5b", style=bold];
    NOGID -> GUTM [style=dotted]; NOLIPID -> MEOHC [style=dotted];
    DEGSPEC -> ADH [style=dashed]; OUTBRK -> FOMSUP [style=dashed];
    OUTBRK -> ETHD [style=dashed];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     19 · CLINICAL ENDPOINTS
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_end {
    label=<<B>19 · Clinical endpoints — what the integrals become</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#eef4fd"; color="#8fabd0"; fontsize=10;
    MORT    [label="MORT — mortality hazard\nstate variable;\ndriven by pH, CNS formate integral,\nand coma depth", fillcolor="#cfe4ff", penwidth=1.8];
    BLIND   [label="BLIND — permanent visual loss\nhazard on the optic injury integral;\n25–33% of symptomatic methanol\nsurvivors in outbreak series", fillcolor="#cfe4ff", penwidth=1.8];
    ESRD    [label="Dialysis dependence / CKD\nEG-specific endpoint", fillcolor="#cfe4ff"];
    PHPRED  [label="pH IS THE DOMINANT PREDICTOR\nadmission pH <7.00 vs >7.20 separates\nthe survival curves more sharply than\nany concentration — and the model\nDERIVES this rather than fitting it", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.8];
    LOSICU  [label="ICU length of stay\ndriven by blocked-state half-life:\nmethanol days, EG ~1 day", fillcolor="#cfe4ff"];
    COMBO   [label="Composite: 'good outcome'\nalive, independent, logMAR <0.3,\ndialysis-independent", fillcolor="#cfe4ff", penwidth=1.4];

    PHB -> MORT [style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    FORMCNS -> MORT [style=bold]; PUT -> MORT [style=bold];
    OEDEM -> MORT; RESPF -> MORT;
    OPTIC -> BLIND [style=bold, color="#b03a3a", penwidth=1.6];
    VISUAL -> BLIND [style=dotted];
    GFR -> ESRD [style=bold]; CAOXK -> ESRD;
    PHB -> PHPRED [style=dotted]; FHA -> PHPRED [style=dotted];
    HLTAB -> LOSICU [style=dotted];
    MORT -> COMBO [style=dotted]; BLIND -> COMBO [style=dotted]; ESRD -> COMBO [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     20 · DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS AND THE TWO DELIBERATE CONTROLS
     ===================================================================== */
  subgraph cluster_ddx {
    label=<<B>20 · Differential diagnosis — and the two controls that make the argument falsifiable</B>>;
    style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#f0f7f0"; color="#95bd95"; fontsize=10;
    ISOP    [label="CONTROL 1 — Isopropanol\nADH → ACETONE (a ketone, not an acid)\n→ big OG, ketosis, NO anion gap,\nno acidosis, no organ injury.\nProves the acid, not the alcohol, is the poison.", fillcolor="#e2f0e2", penwidth=1.8];
    PGIAT   [label="CONTROL 2 — Propylene glycol\nADH → lactaldehyde → L-LACTATE\n→ OG AND AG both rise, but the acid\nis ordinary lactate and the cause is\nthe patient's own sedative infusion.\nProves the gaps are not specific.", fillcolor="#e2f0e2", penwidth=1.8];
    DKA     [label="High-AG differential\nDKA · alcoholic ketoacidosis ·\nlactic acidosis · uraemia ·\nsalicylate · pyroglutamic acid ·\nD-lactate · starvation", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    SALIC   [label="Salicylate — the trap\nmixed respiratory alkalosis +\nmetabolic acidosis, tinnitus;\ntreated by alkalinisation too", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    KETOOG  [label="Ketoacidosis can itself widen the OG\n(acetone, glycerol, acetoacetate)\n→ an OG does not prove an alcohol", fillcolor="#efefef"];
    URXTL2  [label="History is unreliable\ncovert administration, altered patient,\nempty bottle absent → the gaps and\nthe pH are the only witnesses", fillcolor="#efefef", penwidth=1.4];

    ISOP -> OGAP [label="OG only", style=bold, color="#2e7d5b"];
    ISOP -> AGAP [label="NO rise", style=dashed, color="#2e7d5b"];
    PGIAT -> OGAP [style=bold]; PGIAT -> AGAP [style=bold]; PGIAT -> LACT [style=bold];
    DKA -> AGAP [style=dashed]; SALIC -> AGAP [style=dashed]; SALIC -> PACO2 [style=dashed];
    KETOOG -> OGAP [style=dashed];
    URXTL2 -> CLOCK [style=dotted];
    LGAP -> PGIAT [style=dotted];
  }

  /* =====================================================================
     CROSS-CLUSTER SPINE — the six sentences of the thesis, as edges
     ===================================================================== */
  FLUXA -> INVAR   [style=bold, color="#7a3b8f", penwidth=1.8, label="(1) one flux,\ntwo gaps"];
  FHA   -> MORT    [style=bold, color="#7a3b8f", penwidth=1.8, label="(2) pH is in the\nDELIVERY term"];
  CLRE  -> FOMD    [style=bold, color="#7a3b8f", penwidth=1.8, label="(3) EG has a renal exit,\nmethanol does not"];
  IHD   -> FOMKI   [style=bold, color="#7a3b8f", penwidth=1.8, label="(4) the antidote is\ndialysed too"];
  SSPROD -> NONADD [style=bold, color="#7a3b8f", penwidth=1.8, label="(5) a threshold makes\ntoxicity rate-dependent"];
  ISOP  -> FLUXA   [style=bold, color="#7a3b8f", penwidth=1.8, label="control: no acid,\nno disease"];
}
